DEEP[25]=("<i>Avyakta</i>, unmanifest; <i>acintya</i>, unthinkable; <i>avikārya</i>, unchanging. <i>Tasmād evaṃ viditvainam</i>, “therefore, knowing it thus”: this closes the first argument, which began at 2.11.",
 "The Kena Upaniṣad (1.5–6) says: that which speech cannot express, but by which speech is expressed; that which the mind cannot think, but by which the mind thinks: know that to be Brahman. Commentators read 2.25 in that spirit. The Self is not unknown in the sense of missing; it is unknown as an object because it is always the subject. Śaṅkara’s whole method rests on this. Rāmānuja agrees the self cannot be grasped by the senses but holds that it is known as a distinct conscious being. Madhva says even the self’s full nature is known only by God’s grace.",
 "Try this: notice that you are aware. That awareness itself cannot be pointed at like a chair. The Gītā says that is close to what you are.")
DEEP[26]=("<i>Atha caitaṃ nitya-jātaṃ nityaṃ vā manyase mṛtam</i>, “and if you think it is always being born, always dying”. <i>Mahā-bāho</i>, “mighty-armed”, is one of Kṛṣṇa’s favourite names for Arjuna.",
 "Commentators find this verse a model of fair argument. Kṛṣṇa grants, for the sake of argument, a different view. The Lokāyata (Cārvāka) materialists held that consciousness is a product of the body. Some early Buddhist schools saw the self as a stream of moments. Kṛṣṇa does not argue with them here; he says that even if they were right, grief would make no sense, as the next two verses show. Teachers call this <i>abhyupagama-vāda</i>, arguing on the opponent’s premise. It shows the Gītā’s confidence: its practical advice holds on more than one view of the self.",
 "When someone disagrees with your deepest beliefs, look for advice that helps them anyway. Kindness that does not depend on agreement is strong.")
DEEP[27]=("<i>Jātasya hi dhruvo mṛtyur</i>, “for the born, death is certain”; <i>dhruvaṃ janma mṛtasya ca</i>, “and birth is certain for the dead”. <i>Aparihārye’rthe</i>, “in a matter that cannot be avoided”.",
 "The first half is a universal truth accepted by every view. The second, birth after death, belongs to the Gītā’s own outlook, slipped into the “other view” as a reminder. The Mahābhārata famously tells of Yudhiṣṭhira at the lake, asked by a yakṣa, “what is the greatest wonder?”. He answers: every day people die, yet the living act as if they will live for ever. Commentators pair this with 2.27. Accepting death is not morbid; it is the ground of wise living. The Buddha’s teaching of impermanence and the Stoics’ reflection on death make similar points.",
 "Death is certain; the time is not. Living with that in mind makes us kinder and less wasteful with our days.")
DEEP[28]=("<i>Avyaktādīni bhūtāni</i>, “beings are unmanifest at the beginning”; <i>vyakta-madhyāni</i>, “manifest in the middle”; <i>avyakta-nidhanāny eva</i>, “unmanifest at the end”. <i>Tatra kā paridevanā</i>, “what lament is there in that?”.",
 "The word <i>avyakta</i>, unmanifest, returns in 8.18–20, where the worlds come forth from the unmanifest at the dawn of Brahmā’s day and dissolve into it at night. Commentators use the image of waves on the sea: each wave rises and falls, the sea remains. Śaṅkara says what has no existence before and after has only a borrowed existence in between, like a dream or a mirage. Rāmānuja reads it more simply: beings move between subtle and gross states. Either way, the visible life is a short stretch. The point is practical: we do not grieve that someone was unseen before birth; why grieve so bitterly that they are unseen after death?",
 "Seeing life as a short visible stretch between two great unknowns can make each day more precious, not less.")
DEEP[29]=("<i>Āścaryavat paśyati kaścid enam</i>, “someone sees it as a wonder”. The verse is in triṣṭubh metre. It closely echoes Kaṭha Upaniṣad 1.2.7: <i>āścaryo vaktā kuśalo’sya labdhā</i>, “wonderful is the teacher, skilled the one who attains it”.",
 "Four modes appear: seeing, speaking, hearing, and knowing. The first three are wonders; the fourth is rare. Commentators explain that Self-knowledge is not like learning a fact. Śaṅkara says few have it because it needs a purified mind. Rāmānuja says the self’s nature is wonderful because it is so unlike anything else we know. Madhusūdana adds that the teacher, the student and the teaching are all marvels. Some modern teachers say the very attitude of wonder is a sign one is on the right track. Hearing is necessary; the Gītā will say that the knowledge must also be realised (4.38, 7.2).",
 "Keep a sense of wonder about life and about yourself. It keeps the mind open, and an open mind learns.")
DEEP[30]=("<i>Dehī nityam avadhyo’yaṃ dehe sarvasya bhārata</i>, “this embodied one in everyone’s body is ever unkillable”. With this verse the section on the Self, 2.11–30, closes.",
 "<i>Sarvāṇi bhūtāni</i>, “all beings”: Kṛṣṇa widens the circle from Bhīṣma and Droṇa to every living being. Commentators see this verse as a seed of the Gītā’s later vision of equality. In 5.18 the wise see the same in a learned Brahmin, a cow, an elephant, a dog and an outcaste. In 6.29 they see the Self in all beings and all beings in the Self. Śaṅkara reads the one Self in all bodies. Rāmānuja reads the same kind of self, equal in nature, in all. Both lead to the same ethics: every being deserves respect.",
 "The life in you and the life in an ant are, in the Gītā’s view, alike in nature. Let that shape how gently we move through the world.")
DEEP[31]=("<i>Sva-dharmam api cāvekṣya</i>, “looking at your own dharma too”. A kṣatriya’s dharma was to protect the people, uphold justice and fight in a just cause. <i>Dharmyād yuddhāt</i>, “than a righteous war”.",
 "This verse begins the second argument, from duty, 2.31–37. Commentators note the word <i>api</i>, “also”: duty is an additional reason, not the main one. The main reason is the knowledge of the Self. The Mahābhārata has a long discussion of just war: fight only after peace is tried, do not strike the unarmed or the fleeing, protect non-combatants. These rules were set before the battle (Bhīṣma Parva 1). Before the war, Kṛṣṇa had gone as envoy to plead for peace. Yudhiṣṭhira had offered to settle for five villages. Only when every door was closed did war become <i>dharmya</i>.",
 "Before a hard confrontation, ask: have I tried every peaceful way? If yes, standing firm may be the right duty.")
DEEP[32]=("<i>Yadṛcchayā copapannam</i>, “arrived by chance”, that is, unsought. <i>Svarga-dvāram apāvṛtam</i>, “an open door to heaven”. <i>Sukhinaḥ kṣatriyāḥ</i>, “happy are the warriors”.",
 "This is the warrior ethic of epic India, and Kṛṣṇa states it plainly. A kṣatriya who died fighting bravely in a just war was believed to go straight to heaven. Commentators of all schools see 2.32–37 as Kṛṣṇa speaking at Arjuna’s own level, not as his highest teaching. He uses the warrior’s values before lifting them in 2.38. Some modern readers are uneasy with the praise of war. Teachers answer that the Gītā accepts war only as a last resort against injustice. Its deeper teaching is inner, about acting without selfish motive in any field of life.",
 "Some hard situations find us without our asking. Facing them well, rather than wishing them away, is often where character grows.")
DEEP[33]=("<i>Atha cet tvam imaṃ dharmyaṃ saṅgrāmaṃ na kariṣyasi</i>, “but if you will not fight this righteous battle”. <i>Svadharmaṃ kīrtiṃ ca hitvā</i>, “giving up your duty and honour”. <i>Pāpam avāpsyasi</i>, “you will incur sin”.",
 "Arjuna said in 1.36 that sin would cling to him if he fought; Kṛṣṇa says sin will follow if he does not. The same word, <i>pāpa</i>, appears on both sides. Commentators explain: sin is not just doing harm but failing in one’s responsibility. If Arjuna leaves, the Pāṇḍava cause collapses, allies die without their best defender, and injustice wins. The Mahābhārata often weighs duties of omission: Bhīṣma’s silence when Draupadī was insulted is counted against him. Kṛṣṇa makes Arjuna see that walking away is also a moral act with consequences.",
 "Doing nothing can feel safe, but it is still a choice. Ask who will pay if you step back.")
DEEP[34]=("<i>Akīrti</i>, dishonour or infamy. <i>Avyayām</i>, “unending”. <i>Sambhāvitasya</i>, “for one who has been highly regarded”. <i>Maraṇād atiricyate</i>, “exceeds death”.",
 "In the epic world, reputation, <i>kīrti</i>, was more than vanity. It was the trust a warrior held, his promise to protect. Arjuna had won the hand of Draupadī in a contest of skill, defeated the Kaurava army alone in Virāṭa’s kingdom, and was known for his word. Commentators like Madhusūdana say Kṛṣṇa appeals to this because Arjuna cares about it. Modern readers may find honour a weak motive; the Gītā agrees, and in 2.38 moves beyond it. But it does not dismiss it. A good name earned by keeping faith is part of how dharma holds society together.",
 "Trust is slowly built and quickly lost. Before a choice that could break it, weigh that cost honestly.")
DEEP[35]=("<i>Bhayād raṇād uparatam</i>, “withdrawn from battle out of fear”. <i>Mahārathāḥ</i>, the great chariot-warriors: Karṇa, Droṇa, Bhīṣma and others. <i>Lāghavam</i>, lightness, being thought little of.",
 "Commentators note a fine point: Arjuna believes he is acting from compassion, but his enemies will see fear. Who is right? Kṛṣṇa’s next teaching suggests both are partly right. Arjuna’s pity is real, but it is mixed with grief and attachment (2.2–3). The Gītā will later name three kinds of renunciation (18.7–9). The rājasika kind gives up duty because it is painful or out of fear of bodily suffering, and its fruit is lost. Arjuna’s withdrawal would be of that kind, though he cannot see it yet.",
 "Our reasons feel clear from inside. Seeing how others will read our actions can reveal motives we had not noticed.")
DEEP[36]=("<i>Avācya-vādān</i>, “unspeakable words”. <i>Nindantas tava sāmarthyam</i>, “mocking your ability”. <i>Tato duḥkhataraṃ nu kim</i>, “what could be more painful than that?”.",
 "Commentators see 2.36 as the end of the argument from honour. Some note an irony: Kṛṣṇa appeals to the pain of mockery, but in 2.38 he will ask Arjuna to be even in honour and dishonour, and in 12.18–19 he praises one who is the same in praise and blame. So the appeal here is a step, not a destination. Teachers compare it to medicine: a doctor gives what the patient can take now, then raises the dose. Arjuna, a proud warrior, can hear the argument from honour; he will later be ready for one beyond it.",
 "We begin where we are. If honour or fear of embarrassment is what gets us moving, fine, but don’t stop there.")
DEEP[37]=("<i>Hato vā prāpsyasi svargam</i>, “killed, you will reach heaven”; <i>jitvā vā bhokṣyase mahīm</i>, “winning, you will enjoy the earth”. <i>Tasmād uttiṣṭha kaunteya</i>, “so stand up, son of Kuntī”.",
 "This is the classic either-or of the warrior’s creed: no loss either way. Commentators note that Kṛṣṇa calls Arjuna <i>Kaunteya</i>, Kuntī’s son, recalling his mother, who had sent him a message before the war through Kṛṣṇa to fight bravely for his rights. The command <i>uttiṣṭha</i> echoes 2.3. But even as he says it, Kṛṣṇa is about to change the reason. In 2.38 the goal is not heaven or earth but freedom from sin. The fruit-based argument is the ladder; the next rung is fruitless action. Rāmānuja calls 2.31–37 the argument for one still attached to results.",
 "If a goal motivates you, use it. Then, little by little, learn to act well even without the reward.")
DEEP[38]=("<i>Sukha-duḥkhe same kṛtvā</i>, “making pleasure and pain equal”; <i>lābhālābhau</i>, gain and loss; <i>jayājayau</i>, victory and defeat. <i>Naivaṃ pāpam avāpsyasi</i>, “thus you will not incur sin”.",
 "Commentators call this verse the hinge between Sāṅkhya and yoga. Kṛṣṇa has just argued from heaven and earth; now he sets them aside. Evenness, not reward, is the right ground of action. The three pairs cover every outcome of the coming battle. Śaṅkara explains that sin attaches to action done with attachment, because attachment leads to wrong means. Rāmānuja says action done as duty, without desire for fruit, does not produce binding karma. The idea is developed in 3.9, 4.20–23 and 5.10, where the one who acts without attachment is untouched by sin, like a lotus leaf by water.",
 "Try entering a hard task with the thought: whether this goes well or badly, I will do it well. Notice how that changes the experience.")
DEEP[39]=("<i>Eṣā te’bhihitā sāṅkhye buddhiḥ</i>, “this understanding has been told to you in terms of Sāṅkhya”. <i>Yoge tv imāṃ śṛṇu</i>, “now hear it in terms of yoga”. <i>Karma-bandham</i>, the bondage of action.",
 "This is one of the most important transitions in the Gītā. In the Gītā, <i>Sāṅkhya</i> means the way of knowledge, discriminating the Self from the body. It is not the later classical Sāṅkhya school of Kapila and Īśvarakṛṣṇa, though related. <i>Yoga</i> means the way of right action, karma yoga. Śaṅkara says the two are for different kinds of seekers: knowledge for the renunciant, action for the one still engaged in the world. Rāmānuja says they are complementary steps. Tilak argued that the Gītā’s real teaching is karma yoga, with knowledge as its basis. The tension between the paths runs through chapters 3 and 5.",
 "Understanding is not enough by itself. The Gītā wants knowledge to show up in how we work, speak and decide.")
DEEP[40]=("<i>Nehābhikrama-nāśo’sti</i>, “here no effort is lost”; <i>pratyavāyo na vidyate</i>, “no adverse result arises”. <i>Svalpam apy asya dharmasya</i>, “even a little of this dharma”. <i>Trāyate mahato bhayāt</i>, “saves from great fear”.",
 "In Vedic ritual, an unfinished sacrifice gave no result, and an error could bring harm, <i>pratyavāya</i>. Kṛṣṇa says karma yoga is different. Commentators explain why: it depends on the inner attitude, not on perfect outer performance. Even a little gain in purity of mind is never lost. Chapter 6 (6.40–45) develops this into a full promise: the one who falls from yoga is reborn in a good family and continues the journey. Many teachers find this one of the most encouraging verses in the Gītā. Gandhi said it gave him hope in his imperfect efforts.",
 "If you have started a practice and slipped, don’t give up. Every sincere effort stays with you.")
DEEP[41]=("<i>Vyavasāyātmikā buddhiḥ</i>, “resolute understanding”; <i>ekā</i>, “single”. <i>Bahu-śākhāḥ</i>, “many-branched”; <i>anantāḥ</i>, “endless”. <i>Kuru-nandana</i>, “joy of the Kurus”, a fond name for Arjuna.",
 "Commentators read this verse as the key to 2.42–46: a mind fixed on liberation has one aim; a mind fixed on many rewards has endless branches. Śaṅkara calls the single understanding the conviction that comes from right knowledge. Rāmānuja calls it the resolve to reach the self, from which all actions take their direction. The same idea appears in the image of a tree: one root, many branches. Teachers say the one-pointed mind is not narrow; it can do many things, but all serve one purpose. The idea becomes central again in 6.25–26 and in the devotion of chapters 9 and 12.",
 "List your goals. Then ask what single thing they are all serving. Life becomes simpler when that is clear.")
DEEP[42]=("<i>Yām imāṃ puṣpitāṃ vācam</i>, “this flowery speech”. <i>Veda-vāda-ratāḥ</i>, “delighting in the words of the Vedas”. <i>Nānyad astīti vādinaḥ</i>, “saying there is nothing else”.",
 "This verse (with 2.43–44) is the Gītā’s sharpest critique of ritualism. It is not against the Vedas; the Gītā calls the Vedas Kṛṣṇa’s own form (9.17, 15.15). It is aimed at those who stop at the rituals for rewards and deny any higher goal. In the Gītā’s time, the Mīmāṃsā school held that ritual action was the whole of dharma. The Upaniṣads had already pushed toward knowledge as the highest aim. The Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad (1.2.7) calls ritual boats unsteady. Commentators of every school read the Gītā as standing with the Upaniṣads here, while honouring right ritual done without craving.",
 "Any practice, religious or not, can become a way of bargaining for rewards. Notice when that happens, and return to its real purpose.")
DEEP[43]=("<i>Kāmātmānaḥ</i>, “whose self is desire”; <i>svarga-parāḥ</i>, “intent on heaven”. <i>Janma-karma-phala-pradām</i>, “offering birth as the fruit of action”. <i>Bhogaiśvarya-gatiṃ prati</i>, “aimed at enjoyment and power”.",
 "Heaven in the Vedic sense is a world of pleasure won by merit, but it does not last: when the merit is spent, one returns to earth (9.20–21). Commentators note the phrase <i>janma-karma-phala-pradām</i>: such rites give birth again as their fruit. In other words, they keep the wheel turning. Śaṅkara says those who chase heaven remain bound. Rāmānuja says they miss the higher goal of reaching the Lord. The Gītā does not condemn the rites; it says their aim is too small.",
 "Ask of your goals: when I get this, then what? If the answer is always “something more”, look for a deeper aim.")
DEEP[44]=("<i>Bhogaiśvarya-prasaktānām</i>, “of those attached to pleasure and power”. <i>Tayāpahṛta-cetasām</i>, “whose minds are stolen by that”. <i>Samādhau na vidhīyate</i>, “is not settled in samādhi”.",
 "Commentators note the strong word <i>apahṛta</i>, “carried off, stolen”. Desire is pictured as a thief. <i>Samādhi</i> here is read in several ways: Śaṅkara takes it as the mind, the place where understanding should rest; others as the meditative absorption of chapter 6. The point is the same. A mind pulled in many directions by wanting cannot become steady. The theme returns in the sthita-prajña verses (2.55–72), where steady wisdom begins when all desires of the mind are given up (2.55).",
 "If your mind feels scattered, look at what it is chasing. Fewer chases mean more calm.")
DEEP[45]=("<i>Traiguṇya-viṣayā vedāḥ</i>, “the Vedas deal with the three guṇas”. <i>Nistraiguṇyo bhava</i>, “be beyond the three guṇas”. <i>Nirdvandvaḥ</i>, free of opposites; <i>nitya-sattva-sthaḥ</i>, ever in sattva; <i>niryoga-kṣemaḥ</i>, free of getting and keeping; <i>ātmavān</i>, self-possessed.",
 "This verse packs five instructions into one line. Commentators note a puzzle: how can one be beyond the guṇas and also “ever in sattva”? The answer usually given is that sattva, clarity, is the doorway; one rests in it in order to go beyond all three. Chapter 14 (14.22–26) describes the one who has crossed the guṇas. <i>Yoga-kṣema</i> appears again at 9.22, where Kṛṣṇa promises to carry it himself for devotees who think of him alone. Rāmānuja links the two verses: give up anxiety about getting and keeping, and the Lord takes it over.",
 "Much worry is about getting and keeping. Try handing that worry to something larger, and see how much lighter you feel.")
DEEP[46]=("<i>Yāvān artha udapāne</i>, “as much use as a well has”; <i>sarvataḥ samplutodake</i>, “when there is a flood everywhere”. <i>Brāhmaṇasya vijānataḥ</i>, “for a Brahmin who knows”.",
 "The image is drawn from village life: a well is precious in a dry season, but in a flood it has no special use. Commentators read the verse in two ways. Śaṅkara: whatever joys the Vedic rites bring are contained in the joy of Brahman, as all small waters are in the flood. Rāmānuja: the wise take from the Vedas only what serves liberation, as a thirsty man takes from a great lake only what he needs. Both agree the verse does not belittle the Vedas. <i>Brāhmaṇa</i> here is read as one who knows Brahman, not by birth.",
 "When you find what truly satisfies, many smaller wants fade without effort. Look for that deeper source.")
DEEP[47]=("<i>Karmaṇy evādhikāras te</i>, “your right is to action alone”; <i>mā phaleṣu kadācana</i>, “never to its fruits”; <i>mā karma-phala-hetur bhūḥ</i>, “do not be motivated by the fruit”; <i>mā te saṅgo’stv akarmaṇi</i>, “do not be attached to inaction”.",
 "This is perhaps the most quoted verse of the Gītā. Commentators stress all four parts. <i>Adhikāra</i> means right, entitlement or competence: what is truly in our hands is the act. Results depend on many factors, as 18.14 says: the body, the doer, the instruments, efforts and providence. Śaṅkara reads the verse as a discipline for those not yet ready for renunciation. Rāmānuja calls it the core of karma yoga. Tilak saw it as the Gītā’s central teaching for public life. Gandhi called the resulting attitude <i>anāsakti</i>, non-attachment. Modern teachers in sport and work echo it: focus on the process, not the score.",
 "Write down what you can control in a task: your preparation, your effort, your honesty. Put your energy there. Let the rest go.")
DEEP[48]=("<i>Yoga-sthaḥ kuru karmāṇi</i>, “established in yoga, do your actions”. <i>Saṅgaṃ tyaktvā</i>, “giving up attachment”. <i>Siddhy-asiddhyoḥ samo bhūtvā</i>, “being the same in success and failure”. <i>Samatvaṃ yoga ucyate</i>, “evenness is called yoga”.",
 "Commentators note that the Gītā gives several definitions of yoga: evenness (2.48), skill in action (2.50), and freedom from union with sorrow (6.23). Patañjali’s definition, <i>yogaś citta-vṛtti-nirodhaḥ</i>, “yoga is the stilling of the mind’s movements” (Yoga Sūtra 1.2), is about meditation. The Gītā’s is about action. The two meet in chapter 6. Śaṅkara says evenness comes from offering actions to the Lord. Rāmānuja says it comes from knowing the self is different from body and results. Modern readers find in it a practical key to working without burnout.",
 "Next time a task succeeds or fails, notice your reaction. Practise meeting both with the same calm. That is yoga in daily life.")
